    {"step": 1, "tactic": "LSASS discovery + module enumeration including cryptdll.dll", "technique": "T1556.001", "what_happened": "At 2032-11-04 03:18:08 UTC on DC-CORP-01.corp.local (a domain controller), AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe scriptblock invoking 'Get-Process -Name lsass' AND iterating $lsass.Modules to locate 'cryptdll.dll' base address + size. Rule 92210 triggered (informational at this stage).", "chain_reason": "cryptdll.dll holds the CDLocateCSystem function — the password verification entry point in Kerberos KDC. Locating its base address inside lsass is the precondition step of T1556.001 'Skeleton Key' — the attacker needs to know exactly where to patch."},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "P/Invoke compile of OpenProcess + VirtualQueryEx + WriteProcessMemory", "technique": "T1556.001", "what_happened": "At 2032-11-04 03:18:18, AMSI EID 4104 captured Add-Type compilation of a P/Invoke class with literal DllImport signatures for OpenProcess (kernel32.dll), VirtualQueryEx, ReadProcessMemory, AND WriteProcessMemory — the four APIs needed to map LSASS memory and write a patch.", "chain_reason": "WriteProcessMemory imported into a PowerShell scriptblock has essentially zero legitimate use. The combination with VirtualQueryEx + ReadProcessMemory + OpenProcess is the canonical 'patch-another-process' primitive — Mimikatz-style memory manipulation."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "OpenProcess on LSASS with GrantedAccess 0x38 (VM_OPERATION | VM_WRITE | VM_READ)", "technique": "T1556.001", "what_happened": "At 2032-11-04 03:18:32, Sysmon EID 10 (ProcessAccess) recorded SourceImage=powershell.exe → TargetImage=C:\\Windows\\System32\\lsass.exe with GrantedAccess=0x38 (= PROCESS_VM_OPERATION | PROCESS_VM_READ | PROCESS_VM_WRITE). OpenProcess returned a NON-NULL handle (the DC has unprotected LSASS — RunAsPPL=0). Rule 92210 escalated to critical.", "chain_reason": "GrantedAccess 0x38 = the exact access mask required to write into another process's memory. PROCESS_VM_WRITE on lsass.exe from a user PowerShell is the strongest possible LSASS-tampering signal — Mimikatz misc::skeleton requires exactly this access."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Universal-password Kerberos AS-REQ + AMSI tool literals", "technique": "T1556.001", "what_happened": "At 2032-11-04 03:18:48, AMSI EID 4104 captured scriptblock literals: 'mimikatz.exe \"privilege::debug\" \"misc::skeleton\"', 'pypykatz live skeleton', 'Invoke-Mimikatz -Command \"misc::skeleton\"', and a reference to the skeleton-key universal master password hash. The same scriptblock contains a KerberosRequestorSecurityToken AS-REQ probe testing whether the patched KDC accepts the universal password. Rule 4104 + 92207 triggered.", "chain_reason": "Literal 'misc::skeleton' OR 'privilege::debug' in a PowerShell scriptblock has zero legitimate use. The post-patch AS-REQ probe verifies whether the skeleton key is active — if YES, every domain account is now accessible with the universal master password."}
